WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.640
Why can the same touch feel fine one day and hurt the next?

2
00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:10.550
After a mild sunburn, a brushing shirt hurts.

3
00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:14.190
A shower that used to sting a little now hurts a great deal.

4
00:00:15.183 --> 00:00:17.943
Pain from a normally harmless touch is allodynia.

5
00:00:18.983 --> 00:00:22.263
Greater pain from something already painful is hyperalgesia.

6
00:00:23.217 --> 00:00:25.197
Same stimulus, different report.

7
00:00:26.077 --> 00:00:27.957
Its size can be reset at three levels.

8
00:00:28.983 --> 00:00:30.243
First, the ending.

9
00:00:31.203 --> 00:00:36.463
Injured tissue releases mediators such as bradykinin, which sensitize nociceptors.

10
00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:39.227
Second, the dorsal horn.

11
00:00:40.187 --> 00:00:41.347
Here, touch can change pain.

12
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.320
Bang your shin and you rub it.

13
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.340
Gentle rubbing mainly excites touch fibres, yet it hurts less.

14
00:00:49.333 --> 00:00:53.873
In 1965, Ronald Melzack and Patrick Wall proposed a gate.

15
00:00:54.867 --> 00:00:56.167
Picture two cells.

16
00:00:57.027 --> 00:01:01.567
A transmission cell sends the signal up, excited by both touch and pain fibres.

17
00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:05.230
An inhibitory interneuron damps that cell.

18
00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:07.630
Touch fibres excite the interneuron.

19
00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:09.710
Pain fibres suppress it.

20
00:01:10.583 --> 00:01:13.523
So rubbing adds damping, and less signal goes up.

21
00:01:14.467 --> 00:01:17.547
But touch also excites the transmission cell directly.

22
00:01:18.567 --> 00:01:20.607
Rubbing helps only if the added damping wins.

23
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:28.480
In their 1965 diagram, the damping acted on the incoming fibres themselves.

24
00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.420
This circuit is a simplified version.

25
00:01:32.483 --> 00:01:34.783
Now remove the inhibitory interneuron.

26
00:01:35.603 --> 00:01:36.703
What does a light touch do?

27
00:01:42.633 --> 00:01:44.253
Nothing damps touch now.

28
00:01:45.253 --> 00:01:49.253
Touch alone can drive the transmission cell, sending a signal up the pain line.

29
00:01:50.267 --> 00:01:55.927
In 2014, Duan and colleagues removed inhibitory neurons of the dynorphin lineage in

30
00:01:55.927 --> 00:01:56.927
mice.

31
00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:59.550
Now light touch evoked pain-like withdrawal.

32
00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:02.190
Touch input was driving a pain line.

33
00:02:03.233 --> 00:02:08.773
That tests one form of spinal gating in mice, not the 1965 diagram as drawn.

34
00:02:09.867 --> 00:02:11.167
Third, the brainstem.

35
00:02:12.047 --> 00:02:15.867
It projects down to the dorsal horn, and can turn its signals up or down.

36
00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:21.730
In rodents, one group of cells, called ON cells, bursts just before a withdrawal

37
00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:26.050
from heat. Another group, called OFF cells, pauses just before it.

38
00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:31.310
OFF cells suppress pain-related processing, and ON cells facilitate it.

39
00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:33.330
Control runs both ways.

40
00:02:34.333 --> 00:02:37.393
A withdrawing paw is indirect evidence about pain.

41
00:02:38.253 --> 00:02:40.733
By itself, it does not show what the animal feels.

42
00:02:42.403 --> 00:02:44.223
A nociceptor's firing is a report.

43
00:02:45.183 --> 00:02:47.223
Pain is what the nervous system makes of it.
